---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_ii3_199",
  "series": "Band II 3 (1695 - 1700)",
  "seq": 199,
  "text": "<p> [[523.13]] <b>199. JOHANN CHRISTIAN SCHULENBURG AN GERHARD MEIER FÜR LEIBNIZ</p>\n<p> [[523.14]] Bremen, 28. Dezember 1698 (7. Januar 1699). [175.]</b></p>\n\n[[524.1]] <petit> u.d.T. <i>Animadversiones in partem generalem Principiorum Cartesianorum</i>) erhalten. Den Eingang bestätigt\n[[524.2]] Meier Leibniz am 30. Januar 1698 (I, 15 N. 177), die erfolgte Lektüre und eine Weitergabe an Schulenburg am\n[[524.3]] 22. Oktober 1698 (I, 16 N. 124, S. 210). Mit der nicht gefundenen Abfertigung unseres Briefes sendet Schulenburg\n[[524.4]] Meier seine Anmerkungen, die Meier zunächst mit I, 16 N. 273 am 11. Januar 1699 in einer schlechten\n[[524.5]] Kopie (<i>k^#'1</i> ) an Leibniz weiterleitet, um dann eine Woche später eine bessere Abschrift (<i>k^#'2</i> ) mit einem Begleitbrief\n[[524.6]] (I, 16 N. 292) zu senden. Leibniz bedankt sich am 2. Februar 1699 bei Meier für diese Sendung: \"Gratias ago tum\n[[524.7]] pro Dⁿ^#'i Schulenburgii judicio, quod doctrinandi ingenium viri ostendit, tum quod ad editionem <i>Animadversionum</i>\n[[524.8]] mearum in Cartesium, et favorem Tuum et Saurmanni operam offers.\" (I, 16 N. 324, S. 536). Schulenburg selbst\n[[524.9]] erwähnt Leibniz gegenüber die <i>Statera</i> nur kurz und ohne inhaltiche Bezugnahme erst in seinem Brief vom\n[[524.10]] 1. Februar 1701 (LBr 842, Bl. 13-14). </p>\n<p> [[524.11]] Die Zählung entspricht dem Druck bei <k>Gerhardt</k>. Die beiden Teile der <i>Animadversiones</i> werden römisch,\n[[524.12]] die jeweiligen Artikel arabisch angeführt.</petit> </p>\n<p> [[524.13]] VIRO Maxime Reverendo, Dn. D. G. Mejero\n [[524.14]] S. D. P. M. Joh. Christian Schulenburg, Rector </p>\n<p> [[524.15]] Singulares Tibi habeo gratias, Fautor Optime, quod Illustris Leibnitii Ms^#'t^#'u^#'m multiplici,\n[[524.16]] eaque profunda prorsus Eruditione refertissimum, Animadversiones in Cartesii <i>Principior.</i>\n[[524.17]] libr. 2. priores exhibens, quod certe hujus sectatoribus, nisi Veritati Palma fuerit concedenda,\n[[524.18]] negotium facesset maximum, nec forte superandum, mecum communicare volueris. Fateor,\n[[524.19]] quemadmodum ex aureis Literis, quas ex peculiari favore ad me, nihil tale merentem, dedit\n[[524.20]] alias acutissimus Leibnitius, multa in Rem Literariam, Mathesin, ac Philosophiam, non minus\n[[524.21]] Naturalem, quam Metaphysicam edoctus sum, praesertim Elegantissimam illam ac simplicissimam\n[[524.22]] computandi Rationem, quam vocat dyadicam; ita quoque ex hoc ipso scripto me\n[[524.23]] quamplurima hausisse, eaque Egregia. Adsunt multa in hoc opere, quae me hactenus in\n[[524.24]] nonnullis vacillantem statuminarunt; sunt, quae in plurimis, de Vacuo, Spatio, Nihilo, Substantia,\n[[524.25]] Extensione, Motu, Quiete etc. me dubiis ac scrupulis liberarunt; sunt quae in Veritatis\n[[524.26]] tramite alicubi constitutum confirmarunt; sunt denique, quae errantem ac saepe nescium feliciter\n[[524.27]] informarunt. Atque sic vides Vir Optime, me multo nomine et Ingeniosissimo huic Scripto\n[[524.28]] Leibnitiano, et Tibi, quod ad manus meas illud pervenire siveris, esse obstrictissimum. Veri\n[[524.29]] Philosophi Nomen obtinet Celeberrimus Leibnitius, quod Neminem, nec ipsum Cartesium, (ad\n[[524.30]] P. I. Artic. 65-68 et P. II, 5, 6, 7, 33, 34-35) laude sua aut honore privet, quod cuique, Apollonio,\n[[524.31]] Proclo, Robervallio, (I, [1]) Hugenio (I, 52) Malebranchio, ut puto, (I, 54) Archimedi,\n[[524.32]] Keplero, Galilaeo, Gassendo, (II, 32, 37-38) (Haec nomina in scholis Cartesianis, ubi in solius\n[[524.33]] Cartesii paginis omnis coagitur, coarctaturque Sapientia, nunquam audiuntur) Logicae etiam\n[[524.34]] Aristotelis vulgari (I, 75), ex cujus neglectu Cartesium Universalem Affirmativam simpliciter\n[[524.35]] convertendo, vitium in forma committentem, lapsum cum aliis, et hodie adhuc labi multos\n[[525.1]] (II, 54-55) ostendit, suum ingenue tribuendum censeat. Impense me oblectarunt, quae\n[[525.2]] eruditissime disserit Author laudatissimus (I, 31, 35 etc.) de Errorum Origine, ac Remedio, de\n[[525.3]] errore in calculo ap. Mathematicos, de conscientia et Memoria, de Intellectus Voluntatisque\n[[525.4]] consortio ac ordine, quibus ex parte gemina me aliquando etiam cogitasse memini. Perspicacissimus\n[[525.5]] profecto est, et profundus in examinandis Motuum Regulis Cartesianis (II, 40 sqq.). In\n[[525.6]] ponenda <b>Lege Continuitatis</b> (II, 45) admirandus, et in duritiei, seu generalius firmitatis\n[[525.7]] Natura explicanda (II, 54-55), oculatissimus. Cartesium hanc rem ex Quiete deducentem, ante\n[[525.8]] annos tres, vel inanem <greek>taytologían</greek>, vel Petitionem Principii, commisisse cum Cl. Bernoullio\n[[525.9]] pronunciavit. <b><i>Arcus</i> meus <i>Tensus se ipsum restituens</i></b>, qui tamen ex his nunc tenditur\n[[525.10]] amplius, multumque exinde sibi lucis foeneratur. Etsi enim ego aliquando Veritatis alicujus\n[[525.11]] quaedam vestigia deprehendam: pulvis tamen Scholasticus cogitationes perpetuo nimium distrahens,\n[[525.12]] ea solet ita obliterare, ut vestigia deprehensa penitus persequi non possim. Quod\n[[525.13]] Cartesianos alios praejudiciis exuturos, in praejudiciis (I, [2]) densissimis Ipsos, magis quam in\n[[525.14]] pice, haerere indicaverim; quod sensus bene habentes debitisque Requisitis instructos fallere\n[[525.15]] negaverim (I, 1, 4, 6, 7, 8); quibus Veteres et saepius bene adeo sidebant, ut eos, qui sensu\n[[525.16]] indigebant, sensibus etiam, non prolixis Rationum deductionibus convincendos judicaverint;\n[[525.17]] quod fines male ex foro Naturali exterminari, sic a Sturmio edoctus, docuerim (1, [28]); quod\n[[525.18]] substantiae (1, 51) hactenus nondum adeo [clarum] et distinctum conceptum datum esse; quod\n[[525.19]] Extensionis Naturam (I, 52) nondum ambiguitate satis liberatam, nec adaequate explicatam;\n[[525.20]] quod Cartesianos dixerim animae existentis certitudinem cum Essentiae ipsius Evidentia confundere\n[[525.21]] (1, 54) atque meras hic sterilesque crepare voces; Quod Vacui, Nihili, Spatii, ceu\n[[525.22]] capacitatis, doctrinam proposuerim (II, 8-19). Quod mundum unicum et infinitum Cartesianis\n[[525.23]] necessario talem (II, 21, 22, 23) ex falsa hypothesi deduci inculcaverim; non gratiam, sed\n[[525.24]] odium heic loci, sed vero jam diu contemptum, et facile constanter contemnendum sum\n[[525.25]] expertus. Motum a Cartesio (II, 25) non nisi quasi historice describi, atque in ipso nihil reperiri\n[[525.26]] minus, quam quid motus sit vere et realiter, forte jam decennium est, quod prima mea <i>de\n[[525.27]] Occultis Qualitatibus</i> adhuc dum occultata, exercitatio statuerit. Hocque ipsum his ipsis diebus\n[[525.28]] adhuc monui amicos. Perplacet vero hic admodum Leibnitiana ex hypothesibus Astronomicis\n[[525.29]] desumta illustratio. Vix salutaveram Academias, cum jam de demonstrationis Cartesianae, pro\n[[525.30]] evincenda Dei existentia, firmitate (I, 14, 18, 20) subdubitarem. Et certe semper existimavi hac\n[[525.31]] in re non minus, quando de Ideis sermo est, quam in Doctrina de cogitatione, de Mente, de\n[[525.32]] Principio Cartesii: <b>Cogito ergo sum</b>, obscurum exponi per aeque obscurum, aut clarum\n[[526.1]] obscuro involvi, illud supponi jam, quod demonstrandum demum, atque sic peti id, quod est in\n[[526.2]] principio. Durus certe bolus, circa decantatissimum Cartesii effatum <b>de omnibus dubitandum</b>,\n[[526.3]] ipsi Cartesio deglutiendus datur ab Optimo Leibnitio (I, 5), imo Vir Amplissimus proprio\n[[526.4]] ipsum gladio [I, 7] jugulat. Cumprimis autem arridet, quod sagacissimus Auctor notanter (I, 13)\n[[526.5]] dicat, <b>exoticam</b> esse fictionem, quando Deus velut ex Machina accitur dubitaturque annon ad\n[[526.6]] errandum in evidentissimis quoque facti simus. Forte non aberraretur, si quis Cartesium cum\n[[526.7]] decepturiente suo genio alios decepisse velle, et dum discrimen inter Vigilias et Somnium non\n[[526.8]] cernit, somniare diceret. Non est nisi novaturiendi pruritus, quod quando Aristoteles et Geometrici\n[[526.9]] (I, 43, 45, 46) volunt ex certis principiis conclusionem esse deducendam, Cartesius\n[[526.10]] clarum ac distinctum conceptum urgeat, alia quidem verba, sed ipsa non satis clare explicata,\n[[526.11]] rem interim eandem proponens. Aeque ac hodie solent in Moralibus quod jam olim monui,\n[[526.12]] Aristotelis Mediocritatem projicere, virtutemque in puncto collocare cum tamen ipsum punctum\n[[526.13]] (in Math.: Ang. rectus, linea perpendic.) sit maximum medium, a quo ne tantillum quidem,\n[[526.14]] sine vitii periculo licebit deflectere. Alias vero concedo libenter, steriles voces non sufficere,\n[[526.15]] sed Virtutes in practicis practice esse exercendas. </p>\n<p> [[526.16]] Haec sunt Fautor Optime, quae ad Te, aliis districtus, detentusque, ut ipse nosti, negotiis,\n[[526.17]] nunc tandem sero quidem ex hac ipsa causa exarare volui, ne diutius detinerem Magnum\n[[526.18]] Leibnitium, et quia jussisti, debui. Ipse Leibnitius nunquam sine honoris praefamine praetermittendus\n[[526.19]] calculo meo aut assensu non indiget quidem; Volui tamen non assentari, nam fuco\n[[526.20]] non indiget veritas, sed assentiri ipsi victus veritate, measque cogitationes, quas gaudeo subinde\n[[526.21]] similes, exponere debui. An ubique rem acu tetigerim dubito, neque enim hic licebit non\n[[526.22]] dubitare. Utinam autem opus ipsum mox lucem publicam aspiciat, quo quae profundiora sunt,\n[[526.23]] sunt vero et multa et maxima, meditari amplius, et veritates plures plenius daretur discere. Si\n[[526.24]] placet Vir Celeberrime atque Magnifice, has ipsas lineas, cum tribus Exercitatiunculis, <i>de Arcu\n[[526.25]] tenso</i>, <i>Lacrymis vitreis</i>, <i>et Matheseos ac Naturae vinculo</i> ut cogitationum, Emendationumque\n[[526.26]] Leibnitianarum fiam particeps, quia jam semel iterumque mihi tam felici esse contigit, Summo\n[[526.27]] Leibnitio transmitte. Non spero hic male feret, si brevi forte [(]§ 45. P. 2). Ejus mentionem\n[[526.28]] faciam publice, colo enim Nomen Leibnitianum obsequiosissime, cui ut meo nomine cum\n[[527.1]] Novo Anno Bona nova preceris precor. Tibi vero Anni praecipitis exitum felicem et im[m]inentis\n[[527.2]] ingressum fortunatum voveo. Vale, et quod facis, me amare perge. </p>\n<p> [[527.3]] Bremae d. 28. Dec. 98 </p>\n",
  "number": "199.",
  "title": "JOHANN CHRISTIAN SCHULENBURG AN GERHARD MEIER FÜR LEIBNIZ",
  "date": "Bremen, 28. Dezember 1698 (7. Januar 1699). [175.]"
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:199. JOHANN CHRISTIAN SCHULENBURG AN GERHARD MEIER FÜR LEIBNIZ Bremen, 28. Dezember 1698 (7. Januar 1699). [175.] u.d.T. Animadversiones in partem generalem Principiorum Cartesianorum) erhalten. Den Eingang bestätigt Meier Leibniz am 30. Januar 1698 (I, 15 N. 177), die erfolgte Lektüre und eine Weit...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>